{
  "version": 1,
  "questionId": "q08",
  "speechText": "For me, I have not owned a large LLM evaluation platform in production yet. The main reason is that it follows a testing mindset I already use: define the expected behavior, build test cases, run them repeatedly, and check for regressions when the system changes. For example, in retrieval, I care about whether the correct documents are returned and how the ranking changes when we update the retrieval strategy. Because of that, for LLM evals, I would build a representative dataset, define what good behavior looks like, and measure correctness, groundedness, retrieval quality, tool-call success, latency, and cost. At the same time, for more subjective outputs, I would combine automatic evaluation with human review instead of depending on only one metric. So overall, my experience with formal LLM eval platforms is still growing, but the evaluation and regression mindset is already familiar to me.",
